  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Nomada valida, commonly known as the True Cuckoo Nomad Bee, is a small parasitic bee in the Apidae family that relies on other bee species for reproduction. Unlike most bees, it does not collect pollen or nectar but instead lays its eggs in the nests of host bees, particularly mining bees and sweat bees.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body coloration",
      "description": "Typically reddish-brown to dark brown with yellow or cream-colored markings on the face and abdomen",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "One of the smaller bees in New England, measuring approximately 6-8 mm in length",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth abdomen",
      "description": "Has a notably smooth, shiny abdomen without dense hair, distinguishing it from hairy pollen-collecting bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in open woodlands, meadows, and gardens throughout New England where its host bee species nest in bare soil or dead wood.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Nomada valida is a brood parasite that sneaks into host bee nests and lays its eggs among the provisions intended for the host's offspring, which means it has evolved to avoid being detected and removed by vigilant host bees.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
